On Thu, 2005-10-13 at 16:36 +0200, Håkon Løvdal wrote:
[...]
> Title draft: What are the linux kernel janitors doing and what effect
> does it have?
> 
> Draft of points to include: Classify the types of activity done
> (i.e. code reviews, bug fixing, documentation, api conversion, etc).
> What are the difference between the work done by janitors and "normal"
> developers and what degree of overlap is there?  How strong is the
> usage of code analyzing tools? What are tools used for? Which tools
> are used?  How many faults are found? What types of faults are found?
> In what ways have the kernel janitor activity evolved since the start?
> What areas of the kernel are typically affected by janitor activity?
> Are some parts excluded?

Since the typical reader of a thesis probably doesn't know anything
about ther k-j stuff, it surely needs a chapter about the role of the
k-j in/with/for kernel development (and probably something about kernel
development itself).
You could actually try to gather statements from the maintainers of k-j
and other known proponents about "why did you do that", "what does it
give you" and summarize that too.
Perhaps you could also put (more) light on "what does doing such simple
patches help the submitter", i.e. to have a "small" manageable target to
get into kernel hacking and patch submission. This is probably not a
very technical aspect but IMHO quite directly related to the "why" for
many people.

> Does this sound like a good or bad idea?  Are there other questions I
> should be asking?  I am not sure if some of the other students would
> be interested in participating. Do you think it is too much for one/too
> little for two?

AFAIK it is the first writing about it.
Since I can't see two distinct points to write about, it is probably
better to do it alone. At worst, you have enough material "only" for a
better overview over the thing (since there is probably a limit in
number of pages you want to write) other people (in the next year) could
elaborate more on special aspects - expecially if you explicitly name
them in your thesis (which is always a good thing to state where you
actively stopped your work/analyis - for whatever reason).

